// ============================================================================
// CONFIG-RESOLUTION · KNOBS  (framework name lists — the only per-ecosystem edit)
//
// The counterpart of java/engine/config-resolution/knobs.dl, in the matching phase,
// and for the same reason:
// every framework-specific NAME lives here and nowhere else, so adding an ecosystem is
// a matter of adding facts, and NO RULE MENTIONS A FRAMEWORK BY NAME.
//
// WHY NOT resolution/builtins.dl, where this list first landed. That file is about
// CPython's C-level callables — `super`, `object`, `len` — things with no Python source
// anywhere. An HTTP verb is not a builtin; it is a name a third-party framework chose.
// Keeping the two apart is what makes "which names did we assume?" answerable in one
// file per language, which is the whole point of the Java convention.
// ============================================================================

// ── py_http_route_verb(Verb) — the HTTP method a route decorator is named after ──
// A WSGI/ASGI framework registers a handler by decorating it with the HTTP verb:
// `@app.route(...)` (Flask, Bottle), `@router.get(...)` / `@app.post(...)` (FastAPI,
// Starlette), `@routes.get(...)` (an aiohttp route table), `@api.websocket(...)`.
//
// The set is HTTP's, not any project's — RFC 9110's methods plus `route` (a
// verb-agnostic registration) and `websocket` (the upgrade handshake) — so it
// generalises by construction rather than by sampling, and it did: it matched a third
// framework it was never written against, unmodified.
//
// A decorator is a route ONLY if its final segment is NAMED here, so the list can move
// a declaration from "no entry point" to "entry point" and never silently the other way.
//
// THE NAME IS NOT SUFFICIENT AND IS NEVER MATCHED ALONE. `patch` is an HTTP method and
// also the stdlib's mock entry point, so `@mock.patch(...)` satisfies <receiver>.<verb>
// exactly — measured, that was 229 of 253 matches across five real projects.
// config-resolution/entry-points.dl also requires a URL path argument, which a mock target,
// being a dotted name, never has.
py_http_route_verb("route").
py_http_route_verb("get").
py_http_route_verb("post").
py_http_route_verb("put").
py_http_route_verb("delete").
py_http_route_verb("patch").
py_http_route_verb("head").
py_http_route_verb("options").
py_http_route_verb("websocket").

// ============================================================================
// FRAMEWORK-MEDIATED HOPS — the name lists framework-behavior/ joins on.
//
// Everything below names a mechanism by which a framework connects two client
// declarations WITHOUT a call site between them. The rules that consume these live
// in framework-behavior/ and mention no framework by name, exactly as the Java
// convention requires.
//
// EVERY LIST HERE IS A TAIL, NEVER A WHOLE NAME, and no list is matched alone. A
// decorator named `task` and a method named `delay` are both ordinary English; what
// makes them a dispatch is the SHAPE the rule requires around them. The condition
// that carries each list is written at its rule, and the negative half of the
// matching fixture is what holds it honest.
// ============================================================================

// ── py_task_register_deco(Tail) — registers a function as a queue task ───────
// `@shared_task`, `@app.task(queue=...)`, `@queue.task`. The decorator REPLACES the
// name with a proxy, which is why the body becomes unreachable: the producer calls a
// method on the proxy, never the def.
//
// NOT MATCHED ALONE. The rule additionally requires the decorated name to be the
// RECEIVER of a catalogued dispatch method, so a project's own `@task` decorator on a
// function nobody dispatches produces nothing.
py_task_register_deco("task").
py_task_register_deco("shared_task").
py_task_register_deco("periodic_task").

// ── py_task_dispatch_method(Name) — the proxy method that enqueues ───────────
// `.delay(...)` is the shorthand; `.apply_async(...)` is the full form. Both return a
// handle and run the body in another process.
//
// `delay` is an ordinary method name — a scheduler and an animation helper both have
// one — so the rule matches it only where the receiver resolves to a def carrying a
// registration decorator above.
py_task_dispatch_method("delay").
py_task_dispatch_method("apply_async").

// ── py_fixture_deco(Tail) — declares an injectable fixture ───────────────────
// A test runner matches a test's PARAMETER NAME against these declarations and calls
// the fixture to produce the argument. The parameter name is the only join.
py_fixture_deco("fixture").

// ── py_test_name_prefix(Prefix) — how a runner recognises a test ─────────────
// Injection happens only into a COLLECTED test. Without this, every function whose
// parameter happens to share a fixture's name would be wired to it.
py_test_name_prefix("test_").

// ── py_route_table(Name) — the module-level sequence a URL router reads ──────
// `urlpatterns = [path("items/<key>/", detail), ...]`. The view is passed as a VALUE;
// the framework calls it on a request. A `path(...)` outside such a table is an
// ordinary call, which is why the table name is required.
py_route_table("urlpatterns").
py_route_table("routes").
py_route_table("url_patterns").

// ── py_route_entry_fn(Name) — builds one row of that table ───────────────────
py_route_entry_fn("path").
py_route_entry_fn("re_path").
py_route_entry_fn("url").

// ── py_signal_receiver_deco(Tail) — attaches a handler to a signal ───────────
// `@receiver(order_placed)`. The publisher writes `order_placed.send(sender=...)`;
// the two ends are joined by the SIGNAL OBJECT and never by a call.
py_signal_receiver_deco("receiver").

// ── py_signal_connect_method / py_signal_send_method ─────────────────────────
// The imperative spellings of the same two ends: `sig.connect(fn)` attaches,
// `sig.send(sender=...)` publishes. Both are ordinary method names, so each is
// matched only where the OTHER end of the same signal object is also present.
py_signal_connect_method("connect").
py_signal_send_method("send").
py_signal_send_method("send_robust").

// ── py_di_marker(Name) — a default value naming a provider to call ───────────
// `def handler(svc = Depends(get_service))`. The marker takes the provider as a
// VALUE; the framework calls it and passes the result in. A provider may itself
// declare one, so the chain has to carry.
py_di_marker("Depends").
py_di_marker("Security").

// ── py_orm_hook_deco(Tail) — a lifecycle or validation hook the library calls ─
// `@event.listens_for(User, "before_insert")`, `@validates("email")`,
// `@field_validator("name")`. The library invokes these; no client call site does.
py_orm_hook_deco("listens_for").
py_orm_hook_deco("validates").
py_orm_hook_deco("field_validator").
py_orm_hook_deco("model_validator").
